<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">United Duchies of Jülich-Cleves-Berg</h1><p class="st-meta">1521 CE – 1609 CE · capital Düsseldorf</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A union of Lower Rhine duchies formed in 1521 whose succession crisis of 1609 brought Europe to the brink of war.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-duke-of-cleves">John III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>-Mark married Maria, heiress of Jülich, Berg and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-ravensberg">Ravensberg</a>. On his father-in-law's death in 1511 and his own father's in 1521, he united the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-julich">Duchy of Jülich</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-cleves">Duchy of Cleves</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-berg">Duchy of Berg</a> with the counties of Mark and Ravensberg, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-duke-of-cleves">John III</a> and his adviser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-von-heresbach">Konrad von Heresbach</a> pursued a moderate, Erasmian church policy. The duchies were rich in textiles, iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> trade, and the court was a centre of humanism. John's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-cleves">Anne of Cleves</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII of England</a> in 1540, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-duke-of-julich-cleves-berg">William the Rich</a> lost <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-guelders">Guelders</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1543.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The mentally ill Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-william-duke-of-julich-cleves-berg">John William</a> died childless in 1609, setting off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-julich-succession">War of the Jülich Succession</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-xanten">Treaty of Xanten</a> in 1614 divided the lands: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, Mark and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-ravensberg">Ravensberg</a> went to Brandenburg, and Jülich and Berg to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/united-duchies-of-julich-cleves-berg.jpg" alt="United Duchies of Jülich-Cleves-Berg" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: ZH2000 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_United_Duchies_of_J%C3%BClich-Cleves-Berg_(1560).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1521 CE – 1609 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Düsseldorf</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John III, William the Rich, John William</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/netherlands">Netherlands</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jülich-Kleve-Berg, United Duchies</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1565&amp;focus=united-duchies-of-julich-cleves-berg" aria-label="United Duchies of Jülich-Cleves-Berg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4834 403 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where United Duchies of Jülich-Cleves-Berg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5154 659L5145 668M5154 659L5139 643M5154 659L5154 659M5154 659L5229 694"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M